1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:05,800
Issuers, wallets and verifiers from different
vendors are supposed to work together.

2
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:10,180
Interop Playground checks whether they actually
do, and when they don't, it shows you why.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,530
It starts with one command.

4
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:19,110
That brings up the full Inji stack, Certify,
Verify, Mimoto and eSignet, on your own machine,

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:20,870
with every version pinned.

6
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:26,840
First, you pick who plays each role:
an issuer, a wallet and a verifier.

7
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:31,820
If a combination can't work, the playground
tells you why before you even run it.

8
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:38,870
Press Run, and the credential is issued over
OpenID for Verifiable Credential Issuance.

9
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:43,390
Every arrow in the diagram is a
real HTTP message we captured.

10
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:47,430
Here's the credential offer, the access
token, and the credential itself.

11
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:50,580
Now the wallet holds the credential.

12
00:00:51,490 --> 00:00:56,860
The inspector shows exactly what's inside: who signed
it, which fields can be selectively disclosed,

13
00:00:56,910 --> 00:00:59,420
and the key that binds it to the holder.

14
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:03,850
Next, Inji Verify asks the wallet for proof.

15
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:08,550
The wallet presents the credential, signed
with its own key, and Verify checks it.

16
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:11,070
The answer comes back: valid.

17
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:17,440
Every run ends with a report: who took
part, which format was used, and the result.

18
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:20,280
If something failed, it points to the exact check.

19
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:25,430
The playground can tell valid,
invalid and expired apart.

20
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,850
Here the signature is fine,
but the credential has expired.

21
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:31,320
Now the interesting part.

22
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:38,200
We swap in an issuer from outside the Inji
ecosystem, walt.id, and verification fails.

23
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:44,680
The playground recognises this failure: Inji Verify
expects the issuer's certificate chain in the credential,

24
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:47,360
and walt.id doesn't send it.

25
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:49,320
It suggests one change.

26
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:53,240
One click re-runs with only
that change, and now it passes.

27
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:54,840
So this isn't a guess.

28
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:56,120
It's a proven fix.

29
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:03,270
All of this adds up to an interop report:
every combination we tested and how it went,

30
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,510
plus a write-up of the Inji issues we
found, each with steps to reproduce.

31
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:10,880
Interop Playground, by Team Bhrinjal.

32
00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:14,120
The code, the setup and every flow are on GitHub.

